course: dictionary
lesson: 591
title: Availability: free and in stock
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Availability is a noun. It is about whether something can be got, bought or used right now. On a website, «check availability» means look and see if rooms, seats or tickets are free.
explain: Availability can also mean whether a person is free at a certain time. A teacher who asks about «your availability» wants to know when you can come to a match or club.
explain: The word is formal. In plain talk, people say «Is it free?» or «Is it in stock?» «What you see a lot feels common; grown-ups call this the availability trap.» Here the word is used in a grown-up way, so read the words around it, especially «trap», to get the idea.
fact: Availability means whether something can be got, bought or used right now.
fact: Availability can also mean whether a person is free to do something at a certain time.
fact: A good plain-English swap is «Is it free?» or «Is it in stock?»
fact: «Check availability» means look and see if something is free or in stock.
fact: «Availability» is a formal word, so it is mostly used in writing, shops and booking, not in everyday chat.
exercise: Sam asks the shop, «Is it in stock?» What is the shop checking? | *Whether the item can be bought right now | How much the item weighs | Whether the shop is open on Sunday | Who made the item
exercise: The coach asks, «What is your availability for Saturday's match?» What does the coach want to know? | Your favourite football team | *Whether you are free to play on Saturday | How tall you are | How much your boots cost
exercise: A booking page says, «Please check availability before you book.» Copy the noun that means whether something is free or in stock. | check | before | *availability | book
test: A hotel sign says «No availability for June.» What does this mean? | June is the hotel's busiest month | Every room is cheap in June | *No rooms are free to book in June | The hotel is closed for ever
test: Dad asks, «Is it in stock?» What is he really asking? | Whether the shop is noisy | *Whether the shop has it to buy right now | Whether the shop sells cakes | Whether it is raining outside
exam: Which is the best plain-English way to say «check availability»? | Sing a song to the staff | Ask the shop what its name is | *Check if it is free or in stock | Count the shelves in the shop
